cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use contilab in your research, please cite it as below."
title: "contilab: a performance-first framework for continual learning benchmarking, distillation and model merging"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Park
    given-names: Yeochoon
    alias: JonPark0
repository-code: "https://github.com/JonPark0/contilab"
url: "https://github.com/JonPark0/contilab/wiki"
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-23"
abstract: >-
  contilab is a PyTorch framework for empirical continual learning research on vision
  and language models. It trains several seeds at once as a stacked-parameter ensemble
  (torch.func.vmap + functional_call, optional torch.compile and bf16), implements
  regularisation, replay and parameter-isolation methods (EWC, LwF, ER, DER++, A-GEM,
  LoRA-per-task), train-then-consolidate pipelines (Task Arithmetic, TIES-Merging,
  multi-teacher knowledge distillation), and computes 30+ continual-learning metrics
  (accuracy matrix, forgetting, forward/backward transfer, stability gap, worst-case
  accuracy, probing and CKA, plasticity loss, recency bias, calibration, recovery,
  loss barriers, LLM general-ability retention) post hoc from stored raw data, with
  bootstrap confidence intervals, paired tests and Friedman/Nemenyi critical differences.
keywords:
  - continual learning
  - lifelong learning
  - incremental learning
  - catastrophic forgetting
  - class-incremental learning
  - benchmark
  - evaluation metrics
  - knowledge distillation
  - model merging
  - task arithmetic
  - TIES-merging
  - LoRA
  - large language models
  - PyTorch
